[{:seon.task/name "a-unit-measures-the-work-not-the-cache"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.cost/unit-benches` :db/pull-attr measures pull [:note/text :note/agent] minus pull [:note/text] on one entity n times, and `seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current` compares it with the table entry (11.6 KB per attribute today). Reproduction (33f6283, one binary): 11.6 KB on one run and 8.9 KB on the next two; bisecting over 9 revisions gave either value, and the Jolt pin is not the cause (the same on 00a3b321 and 9e42a0d8). Cause: the unit depends on Datahike's caches (:search-cache-size, the store cache) being warm or cold at its start, so the entry is a coin flip and the test flakes. Correct: a unit measures the work alone under a stated cache (a fresh db per measurement, or a declared warm-up that settles it); the two modes are gone over 10 runs; then the entry is re-measured on the release binary (bin/check units). Finding 2026-10-28: the entry itself is fixed (d7dc3bef5b) and seon.cost-test/a-unit-measures-the-same-across-runs proves it; the-unit-table-is-current is now red on other entries: :str/seq-char measures 8 ticks and 80 B per char (table 15, 272) and :str/escape 44.6 ticks and 292 B (table 58.69, 684.55), deterministic from the first call (40 calls: 800,832 B each at 10k). Red already at b6fad58b19 (Jolt pin 937bec61); not yet bisected below it (25010632 was the next build). Update the two entries once the cause is named."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current seon.cost-test/a-unit-measures-the-same-across-runs}}
